// Quantum teleportation: moving a state with two classical bits.
//
// Concept
//   Alice holds an unknown qubit state and wants Bob to have it. She cannot
//   measure it (that would destroy it) and she cannot copy it (the no-cloning
//   theorem forbids it). What she can do is consume a shared entangled pair and
//   send two classical bits.
//
//   This is the circuit that needs classical control flow: Bob's correction
//   depends on what Alice actually measured, so it cannot be written as a fixed
//   sequence of gates.
//
// The protocol
//   1. `msg` is prepared in the state to be teleported. Here ry(2*pi/5) gives
//      an asymmetric state, so a correction that was silently wrong would show
//      up in the result.
//   2. `alice` and `bob` are put into the Bell state (|00> + |11>)/sqrt(2).
//   3. Alice performs a Bell measurement on `msg` and `alice`: cx then h, then
//      measure both. This projects her two qubits onto one of four outcomes,
//      destroying the original state.
//   4. She sends the two classical bits to Bob. The `if` statements are that
//      classical channel.
//   5. Bob applies X if m1 is 1, then Z if m0 is 1, and now holds the state
//      `msg` started in.
//
// Why this does not clone anything
//   The measurement in step 3 collapses `msg`. After it, `msg` is in a definite
//   computational basis state with no trace of the original amplitudes. The
//   state exists in exactly one place before the protocol and exactly one place
//   after, which is why teleportation does not violate no-cloning.
//
//   It is also not faster-than-light communication: without the two classical
//   bits, Bob's qubit is maximally mixed. The bits travel no faster than light,
//   and they are what make his correction possible.
//
// Expected measurement results
//   `m0` and `m1` are each 0 or 1 with probability 1/2, in all four
//   combinations, because Alice's Bell measurement is unbiased. The interesting
//   result is not in the counts: it is that Bob's final state equals the input
//   state in every branch. `tests/integration/teleportation.test.ts` checks
//   exactly that, by comparing reduced density matrices across many seeds.
//
// Mathematical interpretation
//   Writing the input as a|0> + b|1>, the state before Alice's measurement
//   expands into four equally weighted terms, one per measurement outcome, each
//   leaving Bob with a different Pauli applied to the original:
//
//     m0 m1 = 00  ->  Bob has  a|0> + b|1>   (identity)
//     m0 m1 = 01  ->  Bob has  a|1> + b|0>   (X)
//     m0 m1 = 10  ->  Bob has  a|0> - b|1>   (Z)
//     m0 m1 = 11  ->  Bob has  a|1> - b|0>   (ZX)
//
//   The corrections below invert each case, which is why the order is X then Z.
//
// @expect qubits=3 clbits=2 gates=7
// @expect-support 00,01,10,11
// @expect-frequency 00=0.25
// @expect-frequency 11=0.25

qubit msg;
qubit alice;
qubit bob;

bit m0;
bit m1;

// 1. Prepare the state to be teleported.
ry(2 * pi / 5) msg;

// 2. Alice and Bob share a Bell pair.
h alice;
cx alice, bob;

// 3. Alice's Bell measurement on msg and her half of the pair.
cx msg, alice;
h msg;

measure msg -> m0;
measure alice -> m1;

// 4 and 5. The two classical bits drive Bob's correction.
if (m1 == 1) x bob;
if (m0 == 1) z bob;
